  {
   "say": "Alright mate, the query is the token asking, ‘what other words matter for me right now?’ Here it’s the word ‘it’, so attention is trying to work out what ‘it’ should lean on.",
   "reveal": [
    "title",
    "query"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "query"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Now it compares that query against a few key tokens, which are like little labels saying what each nearby token can offer. This is the bloody lookup part: which key feels most relevant to the query?",
   "reveal": [
    "keys_label",
    "key1",
    "key2",
    "key3"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "key1",
    "key2",
    "key3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Each comparison spits out a raw match score; bigger means the query and key line up better. Here, ‘the dog’ is a ripper match for ‘it’, while the others are weaker, no dramas.",
   "reveal": [
    "arrow_q_k1",
    "arrow_q_k2",
    "arrow_q_k3",
    "score1",
    "score2",
    "score3"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "score1",
    "score2",
    "score3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Softmax takes those messy raw scores and turns them into clean weights that add up to one. It also makes the strong score stand out heaps more, which is why attention can get nice and sharp.",
   "reveal": [
    "softmax",
    "arrow_scores_softmax",
    "weight1",
    "weight2",
    "weight3"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "softmax",
    "weight1",
    "weight2",
    "weight3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Those weights are then used on the value vectors, which hold the actual information we want to mix in. Thick arrows mean big influence, thin arrows mean bugger-all influence.",
   "reveal": [
    "value1",
    "value2",
    "output",
    "arrow_v1_out",
    "arrow_v2_out"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "arrow_v1_out",
    "arrow_v2_out",
    "output"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "So the final output is a weighted mix of values: mostly the dog’s meaning, with tiny bits from the others. That’s the core trick, mate: attention doesn’t copy one token, it blends what matters most.",
